  "body_html": "<h1>Von Neumann Minimax Theorem</h1>\n<p>For every finite real matrix game $A$, there exist mixed strategies\n$x^* \\in \\Delta(I)$ and $y^* \\in \\Delta(J)$ and a real number $v$ such that\n$$\n  x^* A y \\ge v \\quad \\text{for all } y \\in \\Delta(J),\n$$\nand\n$$\n  x A y^* \\le v \\quad \\text{for all } x \\in \\Delta(I).\n$$\nEquivalently, the mixed extension has a value\n$$\n  v=\\max_{x \\in \\Delta(I)}\\min_{y \\in \\Delta(J)} xAy\n   =\\min_{y \\in \\Delta(J)}\\max_{x \\in \\Delta(I)} xAy.\n$$</p>",
  "proof_html": "<p>Apply Loomis' theorem with $B$ equal to the all-ones matrix. For mixed\nstrategies $x$ and $y$, the products $xB$ and $By$ are both all-ones vectors.\nThus Loomis gives $x^*,y^*,v$ such that\n$$\n  x^*Ae_j\\ge v\\quad\\text{for every }j\n$$\nand\n$$\n  e_iAy^*\\le v\\quad\\text{for every }i.\n$$\nBy linearity in mixed strategies, these inequalities are equivalent to\n$x^*Ay\\ge v$ for every mixed column $y$ and $xAy^*\\le v$ for every mixed row\n$x$. Hence the row player can guarantee $v$ and the column player can hold the\nrow player to $v$. Weak duality forces the maxmin and minmax to be equal to\nthis common number.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MFoGT, Thm. 2.3.1] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MechDesign.pdf</code>. Von Neumann minimax theorem for finite matrix games.</li>\n</ul>",
